{
  "id": "COMPOUND:21079 - PROTEIN:5188",
  "verbalization": "Palmarumycin CP18 is implicated in the pathogenesis of SCC-3 through multiple mechanistic pathways, all of which converge on the compound's ability to target 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid, a compound that is itself a target of several downstream proteins. The evidence suggests that palmarumycin CP18 acts as a modulator of the ubiquitin-proteasome system, as it targets Ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal hydrolase 5, which is involved in the degradation of 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid. This leads to the accumulation of the latter in cells, which in turn may disrupt cellular processes and contribute to the development of SCC-3. Additionally, palmarumycin CP18 is shown to target A704, a protein that is a target of 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid, further reinforcing the compound's role in the pathogenesis of SCC-3. The integration of these pathways highlights the compound's potential to interfere with key metabolic and regulatory processes in SCC-3 cells, suggesting a mechanistic basis for its therapeutic effects. The evidence supports a direct relationship between palmarumycin CP18 and SCC-3, mediated through the targeting of 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid and its downstream targets.",
